$\begin{align}
\left(y^4\right)^{2}&=y^{4\cdot 2}
\\\\
&=y^{8}
\end{align}$

This follows from the general rule $\left(x^m\right)^{n}=x^{m\cdot n}$.

We can also see this is correct by expanding the powers.

$\begin{align}
\left(y^4\right)^{2}&=\underbrace{y^4\cdot y^4}_\text{2 times}
\\\\\\
&=\underbrace{
\underbrace{y\cdot y\cdot y\cdot y}_\text{4 times}
\cdot \underbrace{y\cdot y\cdot y\cdot y}_\text{4 times}}
_\text{2 times}
\\\\
&=y^{8}
\end{align}$
